Original commit body from 7c0ce5c814486dc88651499b2a386b8ec2a005ce:

npm workspaces. `const { helper } = require('@ws/b'); helper()` in
packages/a called javascript:@ws/b:0-0:helper:external_symbol (tier 3)
instead of packages/b's real helper, and the js_module linker minted a
javascript:npm:0-0:@ws/b:package "third-party" stand-in beside the real
tier-1 @ws/b package.

* js_ts: the name lookup did find b's helper; _is_cross_package then
  refused it, because b has its own package.json root, and
  _disambiguate_by_import gave up on any non-relative specifier.
  _disambiguate_by_import now answers a bare specifier with
  _in_tree_package_candidate: the candidate whose nearest package.json
  names that package (and, for a subpath, whose file the subpath names, at
  the package root or under src/). It answers even for one candidate,
  since that is exactly the bind the guard would refuse; a package
  importing itself by name and an ambiguous match return None. One change
  covers all four call sites of _disambiguate_by_import.
* js_module: a bare specifier that no alias matched but whose package a
  package.json in the tree declares (outside node_modules) resolves to that
  package's entry -- exports["."] (import/require/default), module, main,
  index -- or to the subpath's file. When the declared entry is build
  output not in the tree ("main": "dist/cjs/index.js", four apollo-server
  packages), the package's src/index answers.

Cargo workspaces. Both call forms already bound to the sibling (by name);
the defect was the `use` edge: every Rust `use` became an imports edge to
rust:<path>:0-0:module:module, which nothing resolves, so finalize made
each a tier-3 external_symbol. That was true of `use crate_b::helper` and
equally of every `use crate::...` inside one crate -- no Rust use edge in
the tree ever reached a file. _InTreeCrates (built once per run from the
files the symbol pass saw) resolves a path rooted at `crate`, `self`,
`super` or an in-tree crate's [package] name (`-` read as `_`: a
workspace sibling, or an integration test's own library) to the deepest
module file it walks through (a.rs or a/mod.rs; lib.rs, else main.rs, at
the root). A `use` inside inline `mod x { }` blocks counts them: `super`
leaves those first, so `#[cfg(test)] mod tests { use super::*; }` names
the file's own module and gets no edge (it used to be an external stub).
Paths rooted elsewhere (std, registry crates) are unchanged. #[path]
attributes are not read.

New helper supply_chain.own_package_name(manifest): the per-manifest form
of collect_first_party_package_names, using the readers it already keeps
(Cargo.toml / go.mod / package.json), shared by the linker, js_ts and the
Rust resolver instead of three more package-name readers.

The dependency linker already skipped an import whose dst is an in-repo
id; its two docstring sentences saying Rust imports never resolve are
corrected. Consequence: a resolved sibling-crate use no longer gets a
depends_on_manifest edge to the sibling's dependency declaration, as for
Python, whose resolved in-tree imports never did.

